{{DISPLAYTITLE:Nonviolent Communication}}
'''Nonviolent Communication''' ('''NVC''') is a named interpersonal communication framework developed by psychologist Marshall B. Rosenberg. It organizes honest expression and empathic listening around observations, feelings, needs, and requests. Its four-part vocabulary is a teaching scaffold rather than a validated universal sequence or a guarantee of conflict resolution.<ref name="Rosenberg2003">Rosenberg, Marshall B. ''Nonviolent Communication: A Language of Life''. PuddleDancer Press; author's introductory chapter (2003), https://www.naturalchild.org/articles/marshall_rosenberg/giving_from_the_heart.pdf</ref>

{{:Nonviolent Communication/Simple}}

== Framework and scope ==
Rosenberg's approach distinguishes specific observable events from evaluations; emotions from interpretations; underlying needs from strategies; and voluntary, actionable requests from demands. It also emphasizes listening for another person's feelings and needs, not merely delivering a four-sentence script.<ref name="CNVC">Center for Nonviolent Communication. ''The Purpose of NVC'', https://www.cnvc.org/about/purpose-of-nvc (accessed 2026-11-21).</ref>

NVC is used in interpersonal training, education, organizational facilitation, and some healthcare contexts. Its terminology expresses a normative practice, not a diagnostic classification or a substitute for safeguarding, mediation, clinical care, or institutional accountability.

== Evidence ==
A 2026 systematic review of 31 quantitative intervention studies involving more than 2,700 participants reported heterogeneous evidence and important risk-of-bias limitations. The review rated evidence for some self-reported outcomes more favorably than evidence for reductions in anger or aggression; these findings do not establish universal effectiveness.<ref name="Lazzari2026">Lazzari, Giorgio; Jean, Bérénice R.; de la Sablonnière, Roxane; van Vugt, Floris Tijmen. (2026). "A systematic review of quantitative studies testing Nonviolent Communication training interventions." ''Frontiers in Psychology'' 17:1800455. doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1800455. https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1800455/full</ref>

